-Older Potbot host receipts can contain `custody: linux-secret-service` and the
-legacy `native-keystore` setup checkpoint. A ready revision-3 receipt is
-migrated in place to `windows-dpapi-host` without starting a Linux credential
-daemon or replacing any Operator identity. The old checkpoint name remains
-accepted only so interrupted installations can resume safely.
+Older Potbot host receipts can contain `custody: linux-secret-service`, the
+legacy `native-keystore` checkpoint, and prerequisite revision 3. Windows host
+prerequisite revision 4 forces those existing managed distros through one cleanup
+pass before they are considered ready. The cleanup runs before package updates:
+GNOME Keyring user units are globally masked, any running keyring/prompter
+process owned by `synoperator` is stopped, and any installed `gnome-keyring`,
+`libpam-gnome-keyring` or `libsecret-tools` packages are purged. System
+autostart entries are removed and the daemon binary must be absent before the
+migration succeeds. Potbot deliberately does **not** delete
+`/home/synoperator/.local/share/keyrings`; retained legacy data is left untouched
+even though no Windows runtime path can activate it.
 
